course: dictionary
lesson: 4412
title: A word with two meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A hallucination is seeing, hearing, feeling or smelling something that is not really there, as if it were real. To work out a new word, read the words around it. Here, the clue is «not really there».
explain: Read this: «AI tools can make up false answers and still sound sure; people call this "hallucination".» The words «make up» and «false» are the clues. Here, hallucination means a confident answer that is not true.
explain: The context also shows you the subject. In health, a very ill person may have hallucinations. In computing, «AI chatbots can make up facts that sound real; this is called hallucination» is a warning. Ask yourself: is this about a body or a computer? Then check that your meaning still fits the sentence.
fact: A hallucination is seeing, hearing, feeling or smelling something that is not really there.
fact: Hallucinations can be caused by a high fever, some illnesses, lack of sleep, or some medicines and drugs.
fact: An AI hallucination is when a computer program confidently says something false or made up.
fact: To hallucinate is the verb: to have a hallucination. People say «have hallucinations» or «see a hallucination».
fact: AI chatbots write what sounds likely, not what has been checked, so their answers must be checked.
exercise: «A confident made-up answer from a bot is called a hallucination.» What is a hallucination in this sentence? | *A false answer that sounds sure | A computer that has broken down | A slow, careful answer checked twice | A kind helper robot
exercise: A very ill person says they can see a cat on the bed, but there is no cat. What is this? | *A hallucination | A dream about pets | A cat that has hidden under the sheet | A good night's sleep
exercise: Which of these can cause hallucinations? | Eating a green apple | Drinking cold water after a run | Learning a new word | *A high fever
test: «Chatbots can make up facts that sound real; this is called hallucination.» Why should you check a chatbot's answer? | Because chatbots always tell the truth | Because chatbots are too long | *Because it may sound sure but still be false | Because chatbots cannot write
test: What does it mean to hallucinate? | To sleep very well | To run very fast | *To see or hear something that is not really there | To read a map
exam: Jayden asks, "Is a hallucination only ever caused by a computer?" What is the answer? | *No, a very ill person can have hallucinations too | Yes, only computer programs can have one | Yes, and a hallucination always means the answer is true
